I'm joined today by Donald light who's a  professor at the Rowan University School  of Osteopathic Medicine he specializes  in pharmaceutical pharmaceutical  industry myths which is a topic that is  very very interesting certainly to me  and I know too much of our audience  Donald it's great to talk to you talk to  us big picture when we talk about myths  in the pharmaceutical industry what  types of myths are we talking about oh  well we're talking about the claims by  the industry year in and year out and  that their research costs are  astronomical and they are the principal  reason why prices to patients have to be  so high that they drain the household  you know live income and a lot of you  know poorer families have to choose  between food and their medicine that's  often a come up in discussions about  even with the current administration  about sort of lowering prescription drug  prices and one of the facts that's often  pointed out is that well the prices that  are being charged in the United States  are exponentially higher in many cases  than the prices that are being charged  for the same drug in other countries so  it appears to not logically follow that  those prices are absolutely necessary in  order to recoup research costs right I  mean it seems as though this is a  market-based approach to pricing where  in countries where pharmaceutical  companies can charge more they are  absolutely glad to Wow that's right so  basically every other affluent country  similar to the United States the major  other sources of both research and  development and and purchasing have set  up a systems for evaluating how much  better new drugs are than existing drugs  or worse what they're they're often  safety problems so one out of every four  new drugs leads to serious adverse  effects and most people don't know that  but it's been repeatedly found and and  then they they set the price accordingly  the thing is that the FDA and the  European  and regulators approve a lot of drugs  that are very similar to existing drugs  and then they're marketed as new and so  other countries say well those drugs  aren't much better so we're not going to  pay you much more and and therefore  their prices are a good good deal lower  while here the drug lobbyists are there  more than twice as many lobbyists as  members of Congress and they they work  very closely with congressmen to put  things in for example prohibiting  Medicare from competing for prices for  having competition between manufacturers  for getting on the formulary and all  that leads to prices being about three  times higher than they are in Canada and  major countries in Europe now to play  devil's advocate what about the argument  that okay  different prices are being charged in  different countries but it does connect  the research and development costs  because the overwhelming amount of  research and development costs are being  incurred in the United States  and thus they are totally within their  rights to recoup the majority of those  costs in the United States does that  argument hold any water well it's a it's  a widely promoted view by the industry  and I don't think it does  it it kind of assumes there are national  buckets or silos so if that were really  true then then you'd have to shut down  all the great research going on in  Switzerland because there aren't very  many people in Switzerland and their  prices aren't that high and so then  you'd say well you know we're just gonna  have to shut down our operations in  Switzerland because we're not getting  enough income to recoup our R&amp;D and and  that same kind of arguments being made  here about us prices and us markets  versus us Rd but in fact any good new  drug is marketed throughout the world so  for instance viagra was developed in  England using British pounds  and my point is and anyone who knows the  international markets would say it  doesn't matter you know then Pfizer will  sell viagra throughout the world and so  it's it's based on a false kind of  buckets or silo argument there's another  layer to sort of the mythology around  R&amp;D costs which is that once you  establish what the R&amp;D costs sort of  really are and and as you're pointing  out that that can be sort of difficult  to do there's also the reality that a  lot of those costs are not just outflows  from the private pharmaceutical company  a lot of the the research that these  developments are built on are research  that is publicly funded and that in a  sense taxpayers have sort of already  paid for isn't that right that is  correct  taxpayers pay even more than that a good  deal more than that so they pay for a  lot of the very high risk in basic  research to discover a you know a new  molecule a new way to treat a disease  and that's all borne by the public  including very high rates of failure but  also American taxpayers pay somewhere  between 40 and 60 percent of all the  company R&amp;D in addition because that  huge network of lobbyists more than  twice the size of Congress have over the  years put in place all kinds of  deductions and tax credits and tax  subsidies for supporting R&amp;D and they  make the same kind of argument we need  all the subsidies and tax credits and  deductions that we can get because  research is so very very expensive now  but that means that the public ends up  subsidizing a good proportion of the  company Rd as well what about the  argument that can be made and again I'm  not necessarily making this argument but  it's an argument that's made which is  that listen the development of these  drugs  benefits society at a much wider level  once you account for all of the negative  externalities diseases and conditions so  number one it makes some sense for  taxpayers to be supporting that research  and number two if you didn't dangle the  possibility of massive profits in front  of these companies they would not be  quote incentivized by the market to even  do it and then we would be lacking a lot  of these great medications how does that  sound to you well it's you know if it's  plausible but it's a matter of degree so  first of all we we have a long tradition  in the United States as to other  countries publicly subsidizing research  that benefits all of a nation's people  and even the world in electronics and  and lots of other fields it's a matter  of degree how much of a monopoly price  setting do you allow in return for that  and in the case of pharmaceuticals  we have other related evidence that most  of the research being done by  pharmaceutical companies is not for  important new drugs that are clinically  better for patients but for minor  variations on already existing drugs so  that these new drugs offer few or no  additional benefits for patients and the  FDA doesn't even require evidence that  new drugs are clinically better for  patients to be approved which I find  amazing it seems to me that we should  only approve new drugs that are  clinically shown to be clinically  effective but instead the the  regulations of the FDA which have been  significantly influenced by the industry  allow them to test against indirect  measures of benefits and technical  measures of benefits and and and then  they show that they can do that and then  they get approved but after that  independent physician and pharmacy  groups fine  that few of those drugs are clinically  better so it's also a matter of the  degree to which that research is being  put two things that really benefit  society it's an absolutely important  issue and I'm so glad to have spoken to  you about it we've been speaking with  Donald light who's a professor at Rowan  University School of Osteopathic  Medicine and we're gonna continue  covering this issue thank you so much  for talking to me today you're welcome
 

